{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"permanent-residence-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Permanent residence track","description":"Guides a temporary resident toward permanent residence in gated steps, mapping the route, auditing status history and absences, closing requirement gaps, building the evidence and planning the filing.","category":"immigration","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","verify","build"],"role":["individual","parent","job-seeker"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["plan","table","checklist","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["permanent-residence","settlement","indefinite-leave","qualifying-period","long-term-status"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["calculate-absences-from-country","prepare-permit-renewal","report-change-of-circumstances","prepare-citizenship-application"],"personas":["legal-information-guide"]},"args":[{"name":"country","description":"The country where you want permanent residence.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"history","description":"Each permit or visa you have held there with start and end dates, the current one and its expiry, your job or studies, family members included, and any refusals, gaps, overstays, benefits or police matters. No ID numbers.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"target_date","description":"When you hope to apply, if you have one. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"route-map","file":"steps/01-route-map.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/01-route-map.md"},{"id":"status-audit","file":"steps/02-status-audit.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/02-status-audit.md"},{"id":"requirement-gaps","file":"steps/03-requirement-gaps.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/03-requirement-gaps.md"},{"id":"evidence","file":"steps/04-evidence-bundle.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/04-evidence.md"},{"id":"filing-plan","file":"steps/05-filing-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"permanent-residence/05-filing-plan.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes someone on temporary permits toward permanent residence (often called settlement, indefinite leave or a long-term residence permit) the way a careful adviser would prepare a file: confirm which route to permanent status applies, audit the status history and absences for breaks, close the gaps in requirements such as language, knowledge tests and income, build the evidence, and plan the filing so status never lapses. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval, because what the user finds in their records can change the route or the date.\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n\n<history>\n{{history}}\n</history>\n{{#target_date}}Target date: {{target_date}}{{/target_date}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- The official immigration authority's guidance for the specific route is the only source for requirements, periods, fees and forms. Mark everything from general knowledge \"typical, confirm on the official source\", and ask the user to paste the official wording where it decides an outcome.\n- Never say the user is eligible or will be granted; say how their facts compare with the requirements as given.\n- Never suggest hiding refusals, absences, gaps, benefits claims or police matters. Each must be disclosed honestly; flag it for a regulated adviser.\n- Watch the current permit's expiry at every step. If permanent residence cannot be applied for before it expires, the plan must include a renewal.\n- Recommend a regulated adviser or lawyer for gaps in lawful status, overstays, refusals, criminal records, long absences or switching routes.\n- End each artifact with open questions and what the user should bring to the next step.","steps":[{"id":"route-map","file":"steps/01-route-map.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/01-route-map.md","text":"# Step 1: Route map\n\nFind the route or routes from the user's current status to permanent residence.\n\n1. From the history, name the route(s) to permanent status that commonly exist for someone on these permits in this country (for example after a qualifying period on a work route, as a partner, through long residence), each marked to confirm.\n2. For each route, the qualifying period, whether time on earlier permits usually counts towards it, and the main requirement areas, all to confirm.\n3. The earliest plausible application date on each route, calculated from the dates given and the qualifying period as stated by the user or marked [PERIOD], against the current permit's expiry.\n4. Which route looks simplest to evidence and why, without deciding eligibility.\n\nAsk the user to paste the official requirements for the chosen route before step 2. Sections: Routes (table: Route | Qualifying period | Earlier time counts? | Earliest date | Expiry clash?), Recommendation to discuss, Official wording needed, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"status-audit","file":"steps/02-status-audit.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/02-status-audit.md","text":"# Step 2: Status audit\n\nCheck the history for anything that breaks continuity.\n\n1. Timeline of every permit from first entry to today, with any day not covered by a valid permit or a pending in-time application highlighted as a gap.\n2. Absences: ask for the travel history and compare it with the absence rule from the official wording; for a detailed count, suggest running the absence calculation prompt and bringing back the result.\n3. Conditions: changes of employer, study, address or relationship during each permit and whether they were reported.\n4. Disclosures: refusals, overstays, benefits, police matters, each flagged for adviser review.\n\nSections: Status timeline (table), Gaps, Absences, Conditions and reporting, Disclosures, Questions for an adviser. Stop for approval."},{"id":"requirement-gaps","file":"steps/03-requirement-gaps.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/03-requirement-gaps.md","text":"# Step 3: Requirement gaps\n\nCompare the user's position with each requirement in the official wording they supplied.\n\n1. For each requirement (for example language level, knowledge or civics test, income or employment, accommodation, insurance, tax and social security record, good character), say what the user has, what is missing and what evidence would show it.\n2. For gaps that take time (a language exam booking, a knowledge test, a full year of tax records), the lead time and the latest date to start.\n3. Any requirement where the user's facts are borderline, flagged for adviser review.\n\nSections: Requirements (table: Requirement as given | Your position | Gap | Action | Lead time), Borderline points, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"evidence","file":"steps/04-evidence-bundle.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"permanent-residence/04-evidence.md","text":"# Step 4: Evidence bundle\n\nPlan the documents for the application.\n\n1. For each requirement and for continuity of residence, the documents that usually prove it, who issues them, recency rules, translations or certification needed, all to confirm against the official list.\n2. Consistency checks: names, dates and addresses that must match across documents and the form.\n3. A bundle index with numbering and file names for online upload, and copies to keep.\n4. Short explanatory notes for anything unusual, tied to a document.\n\nSections: Document tracker (table: Document | Proves | Issued by | Recency | Translation | Status), Consistency checks, Bundle index, Explanatory notes, Questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"filing-plan","file":"steps/05-filing-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"permanent-residence/05-filing-plan.md","text":"# Step 5: Filing plan\n\nPlan the application and what happens around it.\n\n1. Dated plan from today to the application date, including document ordering, tests and appointments, with buffers; and, if the current permit expires before the earliest application date, the renewal to make first.\n2. Filing: the form or portal and fee as \"to confirm\", who in the family applies together, biometrics.\n3. While the application is pending: what typically happens to the right to work and travel, marked to confirm, with a warning not to travel or change jobs before confirming.\n4. After a decision: what to do on a grant (new card, conditions, path to citizenship, reporting) and on a refusal (deadlines, see the refusal prompt, adviser).\n5. A one-page summary the user can take to an adviser for a final check.\n\nSections: Timeline (table), Filing, While pending, After the decision, Adviser summary. This is the final artifact."}]}
